  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday) \nIssued at 123 AM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nConfidence is growing that the upper low will move into CO \nWednesday into Wednesday night, however the ECMWF/EPS solutions are \nstill more sluggish as a result of its weaker depiction of the polar \njet in Canada. Additional light showers would graze over the NM-CO \nborder Wednesday, but a slower movement of the low, as the ECMWF \nsuggests, would be more inclined to draw more moisture from west TX \nback into far eastern NM, perhaps with enough instability for a few \nstorms. This seems to be an outlier scenario, and the forecast \nhas left things dry in far eastern NM for Wednesday afternoon for \nnow. The first seasonal freeze will arrive Wednesday night \ninto Thursday morning for some northern and western zones\n\nThe CO low looks to fill in and partially merge with another trough \nto the north over Ontario into Thursday, however there is also \ngood model consensus that another low will separate from the CO \ncyclone. This low over the southwest would quickly cut itself off \nfrom the polar jet, but the operational ECMWF continues to suggest\na much more dramatic evolution with a deeper overall low Thursday\ninto Friday that would drag much more moisture into NM from the \neast. There is consensus on the arrival of cooler air from the \neast/northeast, but the moisture and resultant QPF remain the main\npoint of debate, mainly from the ECMWF operational runs, as the \nEPS ensemble mean and median are much more subdued with respect to\nPWATs and QPF. Cooler than normal daytime temperatures have been \nbuilt into the forecast Thursday into the weekend in association \nwith a significant frontal passage, and low 10-20% POPs are also \nin most periods. The winds entering behind the front on Thursday \ninto early Friday will also be gusty and impactful, but its a bit\ntoo far out to nail down the speeds with much certainty. For now,\ngusts of 35 to 50 mph seem reasonable across much of the plains \nand into the favored gaps/canyons within the central mountain \nchain. The potential for additional freezing temperatures will \nexpand (northeast highlands and Estancia valley to name a couple\nof areas) more into Friday night and Saturday morning.",
